  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>Every page on dogbitesettlementcalc.com is written and reviewed by editors with direct experience in personal injury litigation, dog bite liability, and insurance claims. No page is published without editorial review, and no sponsored or paid content appears on this site.</p>

  <h2>Personal Injury &amp; Dog Bite Litigation Practice</h2>
  <p>Our editorial team leads the editorial team and is responsible for the calculator methodology, liability analysis, and all primary content on this site. This side of the team&rsquo;s background is in personal injury litigation support, with specific experience in dog bite and animal attack claims, strict liability vs. one-bite rule analysis, homeowners insurance coverage disputes, and the damages frameworks applicable to scarring, disfigurement, and psychological trauma in animal attack cases. This side of the team reviews all methodology content and is the final editorial authority on the accuracy of the calculator&rsquo;s settlement estimates.</p>
  <p>The severity multipliers and liability adjustments documented on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> reflect this team&rsquo;s analysis of published dog bite settlements and jury verdict research across strict liability and one-bite rule jurisdictions.</p>

  <h2>Insurance Coverage &amp; Animal Liability Reference</h2>
  <p>Our team also contributes to guide and FAQ content, specializing in homeowners and renters insurance coverage for dog bite liability, breed exclusion clauses, insurance claims process, and state-by-state strict liability and one-bite rule analysis. This side of the team&rsquo;s background includes insurance claims research and personal injury litigation support, providing the insurance coverage and liability-by-state perspective that is essential for understanding the practical limits on dog bite recovery.</p>

  <h2>Editorial Standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary source grounding:</strong> Factual claims about dog bite liability are sourced to state statutes, published court decisions, and insurance industry settlement data — not to secondary summaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Documented methodology:</strong> Calculator severity multipliers and liability adjustments are documented in full on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>No sponsored content:</strong> Monetized through Google AdSense display advertising only. No sponsored articles, paid placements, or attorney referral arrangements.</li>
    <li><strong>Currency:</strong> Pages are reviewed and updated when significant dog bite liability law changes — new state statutes, important appellate decisions on provocation or strict liability scope.</li>
    <li><strong>Scope clarity:</strong> This site provides informational content only — not legal advice or case-specific predictions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections Policy</h2>
  <p>If you identify a factual error — an incorrect state liability classification, a statute that has been amended, or a damages figure inconsistent with published verdict research — email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. Include the specific claim and your source. Corrections are reviewed within five business days.</p>

  <p>Return to the <a href="/">calculator</a> or see the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.</p>
